TITLE:: RLChordContext
summary:: The triads of a space and the group that moves them: T and I on the left, P, L, R on the right
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLTransform, Classes/RLWalk, Classes/RLMorphism, Classes/RLChordSpace, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
A context is a set of musical objects and what moves them. This one holds the chords of a two-type link::Classes/RLChordSpace:: (a triad and its inversion) and one group acting on them in two ways.

Once a base chord is chosen (the first type on the origin), every chord is an element of the group: the chord of root x and type index e is code::[x, e]::. Elements multiply as

code::
[v, 0] * [w, e] = [v + w, e]            [v, 1] * [w, e] = [v - w, 1 + e]
::

and the group moves the chords from either side (Lewin's duality; the two commute):

table::
## on the left || T and I || transpositions and inversions of the pitch classes: the same move for every chord
## on the right || P, L, R and their products || read from the chord they are applied to (contextual): a major chord and a minor chord move opposite ways
::

P, L and R are the three moves that keep two tones of a triad: P keeps root and fifth (C major, C minor), L third and fifth (C major, E minor), R root and third (C major, A minor).

A transformation is an link::Classes/RLTransform::. The same class serves an equal division and the just lattice: nothing here counts the pitch classes, so in 19 steps or on the lattice the group is larger (infinite on the lattice) and the same words mean the same moves.

code::
~ctx = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(12)));
~c = ~ctx.space.major(0);
~ctx.l.(~c);                        // E minor
~ctx.t(7).(~c);                     // G major
~ctx.i(0).(~c);                     // F minor: the pitch classes turned around 0
(~ctx.l <> ~ctx.p).(~c);            // Ab major: P first, then L
~ctx.act(\R, ~c);                   // [A minor]: a list, as every context answers
~ctx.neighbours(~c);                // [[P, C minor], [L, E minor], [R, A minor]]
~ctx.rightInterval(~c, ~ctx.space.minor(9)).label;      // R
~ctx.leftInterval(~c, ~ctx.space.minor(11)).label;      // I6 (opycleid's example)
~ctx.define(\N, [\R, \L, \P]);      // a name for R, then L, then P
~ctx.act(\N, ~c);                   // [F minor]
::

SUBSECTION:: Chords of any size

The space may hold chords of two tones or more: a type and the same turned upside down (link::Classes/RLChordSpace#*pair::, link::Classes/RLChordSpace#*tetrads::). Each pair of tones i, j of a chord of the first type gives one contextual inversion, the one that exchanges them, named code::\K01::, code::\K02::... (for triads K02 is P, K12 is L, K01 is R, and only the usual names are listed). On a chord of the second type the same transformation exchanges the tones that answer them upside down. Two in a row transpose a chord: on the tetrads 4:5:6:7, K12 then K01 is a fifth up, K12 then K02 a major third up, K13 then K01 a harmonic seventh up.

code::
~ctx = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.tetrads(RLEDO(12)));     // dominant and half-diminished sevenths
~c7 = ~ctx.space.otonal(0);
~ctx.neighbours(~c7);                    // six half-diminished sevenths, each with two or three tones of C7
~ctx.k(1, 3).(~c7);                      // E utonal: E, G, Bb kept
~just = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.tetrads(RLJust([3, 5, 7])));
~walk = RLWalk(~just, ~just.space.otonal, [\K12, \K01, \K12, \K01, \K13, \K01]);    // two fifths up, a seventh up
~just.system.commaName(~walk.drift);     // septimal comma 64/63: closed in 12 and 22 steps, not in 31
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: space
A space of two types, the second the inversion of the first between its root and its top tone, as link::Classes/RLChordSpace#*triads::, link::Classes/RLChordSpace#*tetrads:: or link::Classes/RLChordSpace#*pair:: make. Anything else is an Error.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: space
METHOD:: system

SUBSECTION:: Transformations

METHOD:: t
The transposition by a pitch class.

METHOD:: i
The inversion I_k: the pitch class x goes to k - x.

METHOD:: p
METHOD:: l
METHOD:: r
The three contextual moves.

METHOD:: transform
A transformation by its Symbol: code::\P::, code::\L::, code::\R::, or a name given with link::#-define::. An unknown name is an Error.

METHOD:: identityTransform
The transformation that moves nothing.

METHOD:: k
The contextual inversion that exchanges tones i and j of a chord of the first type (counted from 0 in its pattern).

METHOD:: define
Names the transformation a word does.
argument:: name
argument:: word
The steps in the order they are taken, Symbols or RLTransforms: code::[\R, \L, \P]:: is R, then L, then P.

METHOD:: generators
code::[\P, \L, \R]:: for triads; the K names for chords of another size.

METHOD:: names
Every name the context knows.

METHOD:: act
What a named transformation (or an RLTransform) makes of a chord: a list, here always of one chord.

METHOD:: neighbours
The chords one generator away, as code::[name, chord]:: pairs.

METHOD:: leftInterval
METHOD:: rightInterval
The one transformation of that side taking x to y: a T or an I (labelled), or a product of P, L, R (labelled when it has a name).

SUBSECTION:: The group

METHOD:: identity
METHOD:: mul
METHOD:: inv
The group on elements code::[v, e]::.

METHOD:: element
METHOD:: chordOf
A chord as an element, and back.

METHOD:: isCentral
METHOD:: center
The elements that commute with all others: a transposition by half the octave, when the system has one. Such an element does the same on the left and on the right (in 12 steps, T6 is also RPRP).
